 - The Pan/Scan subsystem (#44) mirrors upstream's second zoom/pan layer exactly (the 139-step ±1.02 per-axis factor table with identity at index 82, the 0-1000 pan position with 500 = center, the six size steps and eight arrows + center + reset under View → Pan/Scan with the NUMPAD defaults, the four diagonal moves menu-hidden like upstream's MF_OWNERDRAW rows, state persisting across image changes since `_viv_clear` never resets it, and the drag-pan clamp staying in pre-panscan coordinates so a panscanned view deliberately overflows). Three divergences: middle-button drag SCROLLS the view — upstream's `_VIV_DOING_MSCROLL` computes the cursor delta and re-pins the cursor but never wires the scroll call (an incomplete MPC-HC-style scaffold; riviv completes the evident intent, so mouse-right reveals the image's right side), the Options Controls page carries an "X button action" combo (values 1 zoom / 2 previous-next, default 2) where upstream leaves `xbutton_action` ini-only, and every panscan command flashes the status-bar position/zoom readout (#47 — upstream's own `_viv_dst_pos_set`/`_viv_dst_zoom_set` tails). The mouse back/forward buttons (X buttons) navigate exactly like upstream (`xbutton_action` 1 zooms at the click point, 2 goes previous/next; the NC-variant coordinate slip is kept bug-for-bug).
 - The `stdin:` pseudo-filename (#65) realizes upstream's wishlist note (viv.c:81 — "open a file with the filename stdin: to open stdin"): a piped `… | riviv stdin:` reads the process's stdin to EOF, decodes it through the same format-sniffing pipeline as a file (magic bytes, not an extension), and displays the frames under the literal title `stdin:`. It is a VIRTUAL display — frames with no backing file: the whole file-dependent family grays (Cut/Copy/Copy Filename, the shell verbs, Delete/Rename/Rotate/Copy To/Move To) while Copy Image and Close stay available on a separate display gate, the status bar's `(N KB)` clause is omitted, the display never parks into the last-image cache (displacing it clears the slot instead, like upstream's vacuous blank-display park), and the playlist is untouched — Next/Prev/Home return to the PLAYLIST if one exists (drop files onto the virtual display to build one) and end at a blank display otherwise, never a working-directory scan. The pseudo-name matches one whole word, ASCII case-insensitively, and takes effect only as the LONE file argument (`riviv a.png stdin:` ignores the `stdin:` word — it never enters the playlist or competes for the display; NTFS forbids a real file named `stdin:`, so the name is unambiguous). Three deliberate semantics beyond upstream (which never implemented it): a `stdin:` launch is EXEMPT from the single-instance handoff — the pipe's bytes belong to the launching process and cannot cross WM_COPYDATA, so a piped launch with an instance already running opens its own window instead of forwarding (decision record on issue #65; a later NORMAL launch then hands off to whichever riviv window the class-based `FindWindowA` finds first — upstream's own multi-window race, newly observable through the exemption); the stdin read runs on a detached helper thread bounded at 512 MB (a hostile or endless pipe fails user-level instead of OOM-killing the viewer, and a console stdin that never sees EOF cannot hang the window's exit — the worker abandons the reader at teardown); and closing the window while the read is still blocked exits cleanly for the same reason.
 - The clipboard image family (#66) realizes two more upstream wishlist notes (viv.c:80 — "open a file with the filename clipboard: to open the clipboard", viv.c:105 — "paste dib from clipboard CF_DIB"): the clipboard becomes an image source on the #65 virtual display, through three entries sharing one decode path. `riviv clipboard:` (whole-word, ASCII case-insensitive, lone file argument — same ladder as `stdin:`) opens the clipboard's image under the title `clipboard:`; Ctrl+V pastes it when the clipboard carries no CF_HDROP (an HDROP clipboard still takes the #41 file path with priority, and text/other shapes stay the silent no-op upstream has); and unlike `stdin:` the pseudo-name launch still FORWARDS to the first instance — the clipboard is global, so the handed-off instance reads the same bytes (decision record on issue #66). The reader probes CF_DIBV5 → CF_DIB (raw payload copies) → CF_BITMAP (converted through GetDIBits, which displays any-depth DDBs); the pure DIB parser covers the INFO/V2/V3/V4/V5 headers, 24/32bpp BI_RGB and the BI_BITFIELDS/BI_ALPHABITFIELDS mask compressions (contiguous masks rescaled to 8 bits), and both row orders. Two deliberate semantics: alpha is honored only through an EXPLICIT alpha mask (a V4/V5 header's alpha field or BI_ALPHABITFIELDS' fourth mask), flattened over the windowed background like every decode — a BI_RGB 32bpp DIB's undefined 4th byte (screenshot tools commonly write 0) renders opaque; and a payload from the first OFFERED format that fails to parse fails the load honestly (user-level: old image kept, no dialog, no exit) instead of silently retrying other formats — as does a busy clipboard or one with no image at all.
 - The `/close` CLI switch (#67) realizes upstream's wishlist note (viv.c:37 — "/close needs to work with /slideshow (close after slideshow)"): an armed process exits when a slideshow's walk reaches the list END — the self-advance step that would WRAP back to the first image (or the dead end on a lone image) destroys the window instead, exactly like File→Exit (config saved, clean exit code). Upstream slideshows wrap forever (`_viv_next`'s scan always falls to the wrap extreme), so "finished" is defined as the wrap point in whichever mode the walk runs — sorted playlist, directory scan, or shuffle order — and the last image still gets its full rate interval before the exit. Deliberate semantics (upstream never implemented it; decision record on issue #67): the intent is armed by ANY command-line parse — startup or single-instance handoff, so `riviv /close` forwarded to an already-running slideshow arms THAT instance — and is sticky (never cleared by a later parse, never persisted to the ini); without a slideshow the switch is recognized and inert (no usage box, no instant exit); manual navigation (Next/Prev/Home) never fires it, only the slideshow's own timer tick does; and random mode has no list end, so `/random` + `/close` never exits.
 - The `keep_zoom` setting (#68) realizes upstream's wishlist note (viv.c:41 — "option to not reset the zoom when the image changes") and is the FIRST riviv-authored ini key — a strict superset of upstream's save table (upstream has no such setting or string). Off (default 0) every image change resets the zoom/pan state exactly like upstream's `_viv_clear` (viv.c:1270-1291). On (1) a new image CARRIES the state instead: the preset level and the 1:1 mode (with its restore level) stand as-is — the render size at the same level re-derives from the new image's own fit, and 1:1 stays the new source verbatim — and the pan re-derives from the center anchor scaled by the old→new source ratio (a normalized position keeps its relative spot), re-projected and re-clamped exactly like a window resize, so an edge-parked anchor clamps inside a smaller image and a fitting render re-pins to the center. A display going BLANK (Close, a load failure mid-stream, the F5 clear) keeps the state standing — the anchor survives the blank through its degenerate re-projection and the next image continues the carry chain — and F5 itself carries by a ratio of one (same file, same spot). The pan/scan layer already survived every path (#44, upstream never resets it) and stays untouched. The View page gained the checkbox (a riviv-authored row — the tail rows squeezed to a 16-du pitch to fit; upstream has no Options UI for the note); `auto_zoom` is orthogonal by construction — it resizes the WINDOW after an adoption while `keep_zoom` carries the VIEW state at it, and the two re-projections stack idempotently on the anchor.
 - The DPI stance is PerMonitorV2 (#79) — an intentional deviation BEYOND upstream, not an upstream behavior change: upstream declares system DPI awareness in its manifest (`dpiAware=true`, c-original/res/voidImageViewer.Manifest:19), riviv pre-#79 reached the same system awareness via a first-line `SetProcessDPIAware()` (equal on the primary monitor, same DWM behavior), and #79 upgrades to per-monitor v2 through an embedded manifest (winresource, no rc.exe — same resource pipeline as the #26 icon) with `WM_DPICHANGED` adopting the system's suggested rect (the #78 layout chain re-runs under it) — EXCEPT while the geometry is owned elsewhere: fullscreen keeps the monitor cover (the DPI-ratio suggestion would un-cover it), and maximized/minimized windows keep the window manager's bounds and the restore geometry respectively (the suggestion scales the CURRENT rect, which shrinks a maximized window off its work area; what a cross-monitor move of a maximized window actually delivers is an open case recorded as the QA #15 observation item). What this buys: on mixed-DPI multi-monitor setups the window is never DWM-bitmap-stretched on a monitor whose scale differs from the system DPI — 1:1 is pixel-exact on EVERY monitor (system awareness stretches on any non-system-DPI screen, which post-D2D would masquerade as "D2D less sharp than GDI"). What deliberately does NOT change: chrome keeps SYSTEM-DPI proportions on every monitor (the strip-height formula, the image-list cell, the status-bar part-width math all size at the system DPI — upstream's own system-aware proportions; scaling the image viewport per-monitor is the product promise, rescaling the bars is not), and the jump-to dialog's margins also size at the system DPI — like everything else in that hand-built dialog, whose dialog-base-unit control metrics (a system-font measurement that cannot be per-monitor) they must stay in step with. Two recorded side facts: the manifest is awareness-only (plus upstream's asInvoker trustInfo, the no-op default that upstream also declares) — upstream's manifest additionally depends on Common-Controls v6, which riviv has never shipped (no manifest at all before #79 = comctl32 5.82 classic chrome; adding v6 would be a chrome-wide themed-visual change smuggled past a zero-diff acceptance, so it stays out and the v5.82-vs-v6 divergence is recorded HERE as a known difference — themed controls are a follow-up candidate that would also bring per-monitor chrome scaling, v6 controls handling WM_DPICHANGED themselves); and the `GetDpiForSystem` import raises the minimum OS to Windows 10 1607 for the exe to load, with the manifest's fallback list (`PerMonitorV2, System`) giving 1607–1702 machines exactly the pre-#79 system-aware behavior (the awareness tier needs 1703+; the legacy `dpiAware=true` line covers loaders that predate the 2016 element). A startup self-check keeps the manifest honest at runtime: a process that comes up DPI-UNAWARE (a broken or edited manifest degrades silently to virtualized geometry) fails loud, and a system-aware result — legitimate only as the pre-1703 fallback or a broken PerMonitorV2 token — leaves a stderr breadcrumb. One residual seam: a system-DPI change mid-run (a scale change without relogging) leaves the controls strip frozen at its startup value while the status part math re-reads — the same frozen-vs-live split master had, bounded to the part boundary widths.
-- The D2D viewport stack (#80, [ADR 0002](docs/adr/0002-d2d-wcs-render-stack.md) D4/D5/D8) rides a new riviv-authored ini key `renderer = auto | d2d | warp | gdi` (string key, ASCII case-insensitive; missing or unrecognized falls back to the `auto` default with a stderr breadcrumb). The default is `auto` since #81 — hardware D2D → WARP on init failure → GDI (transitional; #90 deletes the GDI tier and makes the final failure fatal) — and `gdi` remains an explicit escape key: the pre-#80 GDI path byte-for-byte with no stack in existence. `d2d` = pure hardware (init failure drops straight to GDI, never WARP — forcing hardware is a diagnostic request); `warp` = pure software rasterizer (the deterministic golden/CI tier). The stack lives on the #78 `riviv_view` child as a DXGI flip swapchain (`B8G8R8A8_UNORM` everywhere — never `_SRGB`, the 1:1 five-piece) and draws message-driven on WM_PAINT like the GDI arm: BeginPaint → Clear (the letterbox IS the Clear) → DrawBitmap (NEAREST at 1:1, unit mode PIXELS, identity transform, exact i32 rects) → EndDraw → Present(0,0). Deviations and honesty notes: init failure is environmental — a one-shot status flash + stderr breadcrumb degrades to GDI, never a dialog; a runtime device loss (EndDraw `D2DERR_RECREATE_TARGET`/Present device-removed/reset, resize HRESULTs alike) rebuilds the stack and re-uploads the CPU master without re-decoding, and the third failure inside a 10 s window escalates to WARP permanently (WARP failing 3-in-10s defers a fatal to after the paint's state borrow drops — the modal pumps messages); a frame larger than `GetMaximumBitmapSize()` (queried at runtime, never hardcoded — hardware devices typically top out at 16384, versus GDI's 32768, but the dev machine's WARP reports 2^23, so the gate's practical trigger depends on the device) cannot upload at all; #80 tore the stack down for such a frame and rendered it through GDI (no same-HWND GDI/D2D hybrid — the flip interop ban is per-HWND), which #82 replaced with the stack's own giant path (see the #82 bullet). One deviation the design's §13 list missed (external review filed): the ticket text said to try `D3D11_CREATE_DEVICE_DEBUG` first and strip it on failure — the implementation never requests the debug layer at all (a machine without the SDK debug binaries would pay a guaranteed failed creation on every launch just to discover what the code already knows; the flag is a diagnostics tool to add deliberately when debugging, not a default). Backend visibility: every launch prints `riviv: renderer=<request> backend=<effective>` on stderr, and since #82 every D2D stack build additionally prints `riviv: d2d max_bitmap=<n> tile cap=<n> (dxgi budget=<n|unavailable>)`, About names the effective renderer, and the status bar's dimension part gains a ` d2d/hw` / ` d2d/warp` suffix ONLY while a D2D stack actually renders — `gdi` stays suffix-free, so the pre-#81 default launch kept upstream's status bar byte-identical (the `auto` default since #81 shows the suffix; `renderer=gdi` still hides it). The renderer key is ini-only (no Options UI in #80). Two deliberate widenings of the failure ladder beyond the documented loss codes (pre-review filed): ANY `EndDraw` failure — not just `D2DERR_RECREATE_TARGET` — enters the same rebuild ladder (a rebuild is cheaper than painting on a sick device, and the 3-in-10s window bounds the churn; the terminal tier is the deferred fatal, never a GDI hop, so a hypothetically persistent non-loss EndDraw error would end as a 10-second exit rather than a silent GDI degrade), and an oversized ANIMATED image (a giant GIF) used to bump `frame_gen` per frame and rebuild the torn-down stack once each — a #80-era transitional pathology that #82's own giant path removed outright (a giant frame no longer tears the stack down at all). Finally the automation channel: `-dump-viewport <path>` (both `/` and `-` spellings, sticky like `/close` — a single-instance handoff arms the FIRST instance too) renders the CURRENT viewport scene at WM_CLOSE — before the teardown, after the script has orchestrated 1:1/zoom/rotate/fullscreen — and writes a PNG; both the D2D arm (render into the target, no Present, read back through a CPU-readable bitmap) and the GDI arm (the same scene into a memory-DC DIB) dump independent of window visibility, so a never-shown background window still dumps; failures are stderr + exit code 2, never a modal (that exit skips the ini save by construction — it fires before WM_DESTROY, the automation channel's documented trade). The dump rides WM_CLOSE, File→Exit, and the `/close` slideshow tail alike; the one bypass is WM_ENDSESSION (no dump attempt during system shutdown — a shutdown must not wait on a PNG). A handoff-armed dump (`-dump-viewport` forwarded to the first instance) resolves relative paths in the FIRST instance's working directory — scripts should pass absolute paths. And one latch to know: a renderer init failure (or a rebuild failure after device loss) latches to GDI for the process lifetime — a driver that recovers mid-session needs a restart before D2D comes back (the latch exists to keep a broken environment from retrying the device on every paint). Two scoping honesties for the "renderer=gdi is zero-difference" claim (external review AI2): it means zero RENDERING difference — such a launch also gains the one stderr breadcrumb line, the About renderer line, and one `renderer=<requested>` key in the saved ini (none of which the upstream surface ever reads; the saved key is the REQUESTED value — a mid-session degrade never rewrites it, so `renderer=auto` stays `auto` even on a machine whose stack fell back to GDI); and the exe's import table statically binds `d3d11.dll`/`d2d1.dll` (OS components present since Win8, so a 1607+ machine cannot miss them) — "init failure degrades to GDI" therefore covers device CREATION failures, not loader failures. The failure classification is a shared table (external review AI2 P1): the documented loss codes plus `DRIVER_INTERNAL_ERROR` take the rebuild ladder; any OTHER EndDraw/Present failure is deterministic trouble a rebuild cannot fix and degrades the whole session to GDI (teardown + one-shot flash + a queued repaint — never a frozen frame, never a wrong fatal). The #80 scope drew the shrink Linear tier as plain BILINEAR — visibly softer than GDI's HALFTONE on strong downscales; #81's full filter table (HIGH_QUALITY_CUBIC per ADR 0002 D6) closed that quality gap (see the #81 bullet).
