+- The GDI render mainline is deleted (#90, [ADR 0002](docs/adr/0002-d2d-wcs-render-stack.md) D7/D10 postscripts) — the viewport renders D2D-only, and the GDI that remains is chrome only (status bar, toolbar, menus, dialogs, clipboard CF_BITMAP/CF_DIB). The failure chain is terminal at every tier: a startup init failure is a fatal dialog with the diagnosis (an `auto` request has already tried hardware AND WARP inside the one create call; `d2d`/`warp` are single-driver diagnostics that fail outright), a runtime device loss still walks the rebuild ladder (same kind → 3-in-10s → WARP → WARP failing 3-in-10s), and everything the ladder cannot fix — non-loss deterministic EndDraw/Present errors, a failed rebuild, WARP exhaustion — is a fatal deferred to after the paint's state borrows drop (the old "session degrades to GDI" tier and its `gpu_init_failed` latch are gone with the arm). One ladder widening landed with the deletion: three CONSECUTIVE `prepare` failures (a driver that keeps refusing `CreateBitmap` without reporting device loss) feed the same device-loss ladder instead of blanking + retrying forever — the #82 review's AI3 handoff; the count lives on the stack and resets on any successful frame. Migration: a `renderer=gdi` value in an old ini loads as `auto` with a one-line stderr note and is never written back. The frozen corpus that made the deletion auditable: `smoke/golden90/` (five scenes in the byte-equal NEAREST domain — 1:1 in two letterbox colors, integer magnify k=2/k=3, rotate90+1:1), generated by the GDI arm on the eve of the deletion with each frame verified gdi-dump == warp-dump byte-identical; `smoke81` now asserts the warp (and hardware, where it matches) arm reproduces them byte-for-byte, and the rotate scene regenerates its fixture per run (Edit→Rotate's shell verb rewrites the file on disk — documented #43 behavior that poisoned the freeze TWICE: once within the rot90 scene itself, caught at freeze time by the per-arm byte compare; once ACROSS scenes — the original white-background s5 golden was frozen from the shared fixture after two rotations had rewritten it into a 180° turn, invisible to the bbox and both-arms-byte-equal oracles (both arms read the same poisoned file) and caught only by the smoke's content-model compare of the dump against the upright HashSource model, then refrozen from a clean per-scene fixture — the lesson being that bbox and byte compares do not certify content identity; a model compare does). The close-time tile stats line lost its `display=` field (the cpu_display ledger class counted the GDI face's DIB, which no longer exists — twelve fields now), and the resident-tile store is a HashMap (the O(n²) find/retain scans on forced `-tile` diagnostic frames, ~33,750 quads, dropped to O(1) — the #82 review's other in-record handoff). Deletion criteria honesty: the written "one stable release cycle" criterion was re-ruled with the user (2026-09-21, [issue #90](https://github.com/jaredshuai/riviv/issues/90#issuecomment-5754329845)) — the repo ships no releases, so the equivalent evidence is the auto default's master usage plus the environment matrix (physical GPU / WARP on the dev machine, RDP and Hyper-V by a one-click QA script, each run before and after the deletion) plus the frozen corpus; the RDP/Hyper-V logs are the merge gate.
 
 
